  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a predictive, ex-ante simulation over a 75-year horizon, which relies on assumptions about long-term marginal material supplies and stable ecosystem service generation.",
      "what": "This study models a large-scale land restoration program covering approximately 200 hectares in Turin, Italy, and its surrounding municipalities using an ex-ante, time-dependent Consequential Life Cycle Assessment. The simulation evaluates a technosol-based nature-based solution involving urban tree and shrub planting over a 15-year deployment phase and a 60-year operational lifetime. The model balances the environmental impacts of material supply, transport, and technosol production against the long-term benefits of carbon sequestration and air pollution removal.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that large-scale urban soil restoration projects carry heavy upfront environmental burdens that delay their net-positive contributions, showing that carbon break-even takes 45 years and air quality benefits take 61 years to materialize."
    }
